  "summary": "Egyptian Christian painting of frontal wide-eyed saints, from encaustic portraits to monastery murals.",
  "description": "Coptic iconography grows from Roman-Egyptian funerary portraits into one of Christianity's oldest continuous image traditions: wide, staring eyes; frontal flattened bodies; bold outline; and strong local color characterize saints, monks and Christ in monasteries like Saint Anthony's and Bawit, and in Nubian cathedral murals. Wood panels, textile fragments and wall paintings share the same expressive geometry. The style survived Arab rule in monastic isolation and was consciously revived in nineteenth- and twentieth-century Coptic church decoration, notably by Isaac Fanous.",
